it's it's very easy to be seduced by  doing things efficiently trying to do a  lot of things well I don't believe  that's the most important thing the most  important thing is picking the right  things to do in the first place and  that's being effective versus just being  efficient so within a start-up its  success I don't think it's measured by  how many things you can do at once its  measured by how effectively you're able  to pick the one or two things into the  highest priority and there's always  going to be a seduction to get  distracted and focus on twenty things at  once and the most effective  entrepreneurs I know are very good at  focusing on one or two things and either  eliminating the rest with delegating to  other people or outsourcing it so it's  not done within the team but it's  actually two third parties so  entrepreneurs tend to miscalculate is  everything what I mean by that is  optimism has a place but I think even  more so for the first time entrepreneur  you need to be pragmatically pessimistic  what I mean by that is you need to  define all the worst-case scenarios in  terms of financial loss time loss etc  look at what you will learn if that  happens and accept and come to terms  with that before you ever start if you  don't do that and you go straight into  battling the world trying to conquer the  world with rose-colored glasses on the  first time you hit a major hiccup you're  gonna become really demoralized and you  will quit so optimism all that rah rah  rah  that's awesome but you need to hope for  the best and prepare for the worst  before you ever get started I always  recommend that you just watch two  amazing clips from Tim Ferriss take the  next three minutes to sit quietly and  think about how you can apply them to  your business and your life believe  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]
 

